WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.800 --> 00:00:02.201
-Oh, man.
-Hey!

00:00:02.201 --> 00:00:04.670
-I'm so happy you're here,
and I loved singing with you.

00:00:04.670 --> 00:00:06.272
Man, oh, man.
We've been --

00:00:06.272 --> 00:00:07.941
We've been doing this for a
while now, me and you singing.

00:00:07.941 --> 00:00:12.111
-We make a great team.
-We really do, bud.

00:00:12.111 --> 00:00:15.681
Is it true that your dream
was to be a backup singer?

00:00:15.681 --> 00:00:18.150
-Yes, and that died.
Yeah, that -- Yeah.

00:00:18.150 --> 00:00:21.453
[ Both laugh ]
-That was my dream.

00:00:21.453 --> 00:00:23.022
'Cause I thought
it'd be so cool.

00:00:23.022 --> 00:00:26.659
I love so many types of artists
and all different genres,

00:00:26.659 --> 00:00:29.628
so I thought it'd be cool to get
to travel with different artists

00:00:29.628 --> 00:00:32.565
and go all over the world and
sing different genres of music.

00:00:32.565 --> 00:00:34.067
So that didn't work out.

00:00:34.067 --> 00:00:37.203
-No. Did you ever sing backup
when you were starting out?

00:00:37.203 --> 00:00:40.239
-No, I sang -- I mean, I sang
with people all the time, like,

00:00:40.239 --> 00:00:41.774
growing up, like, sang in
different groups and stuff,

00:00:41.774 --> 00:00:45.244
but as far like
being paid professionally, no,

00:00:45.244 --> 00:00:47.914
I just did, like, demo work.
I was, like, a demo singer.

00:00:47.914 --> 00:00:50.283
-I would freak out
if I saw you as a back--

00:00:50.283 --> 00:00:53.352
I'm, like --
What? Who's that?

00:00:53.352 --> 00:00:56.055
-I loved it, though.
So, Reba McEntire and I

00:00:56.055 --> 00:00:58.691
actually did a tour together,
and it was fun,

00:00:58.691 --> 00:01:01.127
because we switched, and every
time one would do their song,

00:01:01.127 --> 00:01:02.895
we'd sing backup for them
on the song.

00:01:02.895 --> 00:01:05.131
Like, she would sing
my pop-rock stuff,

00:01:05.131 --> 00:01:08.001
and I would sing her stuff,
and we'd be background singers,

00:01:08.001 --> 00:01:09.902
basically, for those songs.
It was so fun.

00:01:09.902 --> 00:01:11.637
-With Reba?
-Yeah.

00:01:11.637 --> 00:01:14.307
-That's legendary.
-Yeah.

00:01:14.307 --> 00:01:17.277
-Well, you sing all kinds
of songs on your show

00:01:17.277 --> 00:01:20.379
in your Kellyoke segment,
which has blown up.

00:01:20.379 --> 00:01:21.748
It is the biggest thing.

00:01:21.748 --> 00:01:24.717
I mean, I love that, because I
remember talking to you

00:01:24.717 --> 00:01:26.486
right before you were starting
the show, and you're like,

00:01:26.486 --> 00:01:29.055
"Yeah, I have a great band,
I might sing,

00:01:29.055 --> 00:01:32.992
I might do a thing," and you had
it all planned out, and I go,

00:01:32.992 --> 00:01:35.861
"All right, man," and, dude,
you really did it.

00:01:35.861 --> 00:01:37.864
And you're crushing it.
I mean, it's --

00:01:37.864 --> 00:01:40.599
-Well, I think ignorance
is bliss, Jimmy.

00:01:40.599 --> 00:01:42.335
I had no idea
what the hell I was doing.

00:01:42.335 --> 00:01:44.003
So, like, everybody was like,

00:01:44.003 --> 00:01:45.705
"You should do a show,"
and I was like, "Okay."

00:01:45.705 --> 00:01:48.574
Like, I didn't have a plan,
so I thought,

00:01:48.574 --> 00:01:51.610
"Well, we should incorporate
music, obviously."

00:01:51.610 --> 00:01:53.846
-But then,
you won an Emmy for it.

00:01:53.846 --> 00:01:55.615
-I did!
-Congratulations.

00:01:55.615 --> 00:01:56.782
-Oh, my gosh.

00:01:56.782 --> 00:01:59.018
You have to hear
how I found out I won.

00:01:59.018 --> 00:02:02.055
So, everything's weird now,
like awards shows.

00:02:02.055 --> 00:02:03.389
Everything's different now.

00:02:03.389 --> 00:02:06.125
So you it's not, like, normal,
and usually you're there.

00:02:06.125 --> 00:02:11.164
So I have had a really different
kind of couple of months

00:02:11.164 --> 00:02:14.000
than what maybe I was expecting
my future to look like

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:15.568
a few months ago.

00:02:15.568 --> 00:02:18.872
And so, I'm with my kids, and
I'm really present with them,

00:02:18.872 --> 00:02:21.940
I'm kind of single-momming it
now, so I'm like, you know,

00:02:21.940 --> 00:02:23.276
with Legos, I'm coloring,

00:02:23.276 --> 00:02:25.178
I never have my phone on,
never watch TV.

00:02:25.178 --> 00:02:28.348
Totally forgot that the Emmys
were -- just totally --

00:02:28.348 --> 00:02:30.516
I blanked 'cause I'm just so
hyper-focused,

00:02:30.516 --> 00:02:32.752
and there's a lot
going on in my life.

00:02:32.752 --> 00:02:36.556
And so, it's kind of been
a rough month for me

00:02:36.556 --> 00:02:38.991
at that point,
and my executive producer

00:02:38.991 --> 00:02:42.027
just calls me and like,
out of nowhere for me,

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:43.996
I'm just sitting there
playing Legos, and she was like,

00:02:43.996 --> 00:02:45.598
"You're the best host."

00:02:45.598 --> 00:02:47.500
And I was like --
And I almost started crying.

00:02:47.500 --> 00:02:49.602
I was like, "Thank you so much."

00:02:49.602 --> 00:02:51.370
I was like --
-"You are so sweet!"

00:02:51.370 --> 00:02:54.073
-"You're so nice!"
I was like, "You're so --

00:02:54.073 --> 00:02:56.241
This is a kind thing
to do, Alex,

00:02:56.241 --> 00:02:59.112
to just call someone and tell
them they're good at something.

00:02:59.112 --> 00:03:02.248
I needed that."
And she was like, "No, you won,

00:03:02.248 --> 00:03:04.817
at the Emmys,"
and I was like, "What?!"

00:03:04.817 --> 00:03:06.853
And then my kids were like,
"You won!"

00:03:06.853 --> 00:03:08.521
They were, like, sitting there,
and they were so excited.

00:03:08.521 --> 00:03:10.022
They had no idea
why they're excited,

00:03:10.022 --> 00:03:12.658
but they were freaking out.
Yeah, that was how I found out.

00:03:12.658 --> 00:03:15.895
-That's so cool.
What do you think now?

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:18.564
You're back in the studio,
right?

00:03:18.564 --> 00:03:20.800
-Yes.
-Which is --

00:03:20.800 --> 00:03:22.201
How's it like for you?
I know it's --

00:03:22.201 --> 00:03:24.671
You're coming back
September 21st.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:27.840
Does it feel odd to you,
going back in the studio?

00:03:27.840 --> 00:03:30.676
Is it safe?
Obviously, it's safe, but...

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:32.378
-I don't know --
Yes, oh, my gosh.

00:03:32.378 --> 00:03:35.247
I am the safest person around.

00:03:35.247 --> 00:03:37.650
'Cause, like, "The Voice," like,
other jobs, this job --

00:03:37.650 --> 00:03:40.286
I have to take a test for all
of them, so I take, like --

00:03:40.286 --> 00:03:42.588
Carson Daly and I -- we take
like eight tests a week.

00:03:42.588 --> 00:03:44.356
-Yeah.
-We are very, very safe,

00:03:44.356 --> 00:03:46.258
and the all guidelines --
you get it.

00:03:46.258 --> 00:03:49.795
It's very different.
But I don't know how you feel,

00:03:49.795 --> 00:03:52.631
but I, because my show actually
ran throughout the summer

00:03:52.631 --> 00:03:55.234
because we were trying
to finish out my first season.

00:03:55.234 --> 00:03:57.603
And it's been running
this whole time, honestly.

00:03:57.603 --> 00:04:01.306
And I adapt
pretty easily in life,

00:04:01.306 --> 00:04:03.576
so I didn't really --

00:04:03.576 --> 00:04:05.011
Like, everybody was like,
"Oh, is it weird singing?

00:04:05.011 --> 00:04:06.446
There's no people there,"
and I was like,

00:04:06.446 --> 00:04:08.548
"Well, no, 'cause I sing in
a studio all the time anyway."

00:04:08.548 --> 00:04:10.349
And then they're like,
"Oh, is it weird interviewing?"

00:04:10.349 --> 00:04:12.051
And I was like, "Well, it really
was just my first time

00:04:12.051 --> 00:04:14.354
doing this anyways, so I didn't
really know what the norm was."

00:04:14.354 --> 00:04:16.321
So I was just kind of like,

00:04:16.321 --> 00:04:18.624
"Yeah, however we're gonna
do it, let's do it."

00:04:18.624 --> 00:04:20.993
So it hasn't really bothered me
either way,

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:22.895
and being back in the studio,
it's kind of cool

00:04:22.895 --> 00:04:24.263
'cause we have
a virtual audience,

00:04:24.263 --> 00:04:26.632
and they're from all over
the world, and so --

00:04:26.632 --> 00:04:29.302
and they're all there, like,
in the audience seats.

00:04:29.302 --> 00:04:30.970
And we have guests, like,
there in person

00:04:30.970 --> 00:04:32.872
and also still coming virtually,

00:04:32.872 --> 00:04:34.840
and we kind of sit the screen
next to me.

00:04:34.840 --> 00:04:37.910
So it's interesting,
and it's definitely

00:04:37.910 --> 00:04:40.913
technical-difficulty hell
sometimes,

00:04:40.913 --> 00:04:43.315
but it's worth it, though.
It's worth it.

00:04:43.315 --> 00:04:46.852
-Yeah. So, the audience is there
so you can actually see

00:04:46.852 --> 00:04:48.521
people in the audience --
-Yeah.

00:04:48.521 --> 00:04:51.557
-I play off of my audience, and
they're from, like, everywhere.

00:04:51.557 --> 00:04:52.959
That's kind of the cool thing

00:04:52.959 --> 00:04:54.794
is our whole show
is about connections.

00:04:54.794 --> 00:04:56.995
So the cool thing is,
we really are connecting,

00:04:56.995 --> 00:04:58.797
like, all over the globe.

00:04:58.797 --> 00:05:00.533
-That is unbelievable.

00:05:00.533 --> 00:05:02.868
And are they doing the same
thing on "The Voice"?

00:05:02.868 --> 00:05:04.069
I know you're coming back.

00:05:04.069 --> 00:05:05.704
-Yeah, they're doing it
on "The Voice," too.

00:05:05.704 --> 00:05:08.474
It's not as -- It can't be
as interactive on "The Voice"

00:05:08.474 --> 00:05:10.642
just because of all
the elements in play.

00:05:10.642 --> 00:05:12.645
It's a little more interactive
on the TV show,

00:05:12.645 --> 00:05:14.480
but on "The Voice," they do.

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.316
They have a huge thing
in the screen,

00:05:17.316 --> 00:05:20.185
and we can interact with them.
It's just a little --

00:05:20.185 --> 00:05:22.521
You know, it's more about
the artist, obviously, there,

00:05:22.521 --> 00:05:24.823
and it's more of, like,
they're just watching,

00:05:24.823 --> 00:05:26.692
and we chime in
every now and then with them.

00:05:26.692 --> 00:05:29.695
But it's interesting, though,
because even doing "The Voice,"

00:05:29.695 --> 00:05:32.431
you hear everything.
It's far more intimate.

00:05:32.431 --> 00:05:34.267
It's really different,
and it's kind of cool.

00:05:34.267 --> 00:05:36.802
All the coaches are kinda like,
"This is cool."

00:05:36.802 --> 00:05:39.872
-You can hear everyone's --
actually hear their voice.

00:05:39.872 --> 00:05:41.607
-No, well, I just mean,
you can hear --

00:05:41.607 --> 00:05:43.876
Like, the crowd goes wild
on certain parts,

00:05:43.876 --> 00:05:46.279
and you're like, "Wait!
We can't hear everything."

00:05:46.279 --> 00:05:47.613
-Yeah.
-And then my mom will call me

00:05:47.613 --> 00:05:49.448
and be like, "Why did you say
they did good?

00:05:49.448 --> 00:05:51.550
They were so off-key."
And I was --

00:05:51.550 --> 00:05:54.286
-I love mom.
I love mom so much.

00:05:54.286 --> 00:05:56.689
Come on. What would you do
without your mom?

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:59.892
Well, congrats on "The Voice"
in October,

00:05:59.892 --> 00:06:03.096
you have your show coming back
September 21st.

00:06:03.096 --> 00:06:04.763
Pal, you're one of my faves.

00:06:04.763 --> 00:06:06.365
Thank you so much
for doing the show.

00:06:06.365 --> 00:06:07.766
I appreciate it.

00:06:07.766 --> 00:06:09.869
We always have fun together,
so thank you for doing that.

